doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22043513
repository-code: 'https://github.com/furqan5/ion-battery-pinn-core-temperature'
abstract: >-
  Core temperature of cylindrical lithium-ion cells from surface, ambient and
  current channels, validated against measured core thermocouples. The
  quasi-steady relation T_core = T_surf + G (T_surf - T_inf), with G = Bi/2,
  is calibrated against a core thermocouple on one drive cycle of an A123 26650
  cell and reproduces the other with 0.130 K RMSE; a Monte Carlo uncertainty
  evaluation following JCGM 100 and JCGM 101 gives 0.24 K standard uncertainty
  at peak gradient. The relation's dynamic error is derived in closed form and
  does not depend on Bi. Identifying Bi from the surface alone amplifies errors
  in heat capacity and heat input by 1 + 4/Bi; version 1.0.0 used a heat
  capacity identified with both thermocouples, which version 1.1.0 corrects.
  LG M50 21700 cells with embedded thermocouples confirm the relation and show
  that the calibrated gain depends on the surface-sensor installation. A
  transient finite-volume inverse and an inverse physics-informed neural network
  are less accurate on the same information. A companion phase-change study and
  a Cramer-Rao identifiability analysis are included.
keywords:
  - lithium-ion battery
  - core temperature estimation
  - inverse problems
  - physics-informed neural networks
  - identifiability
  - Cramer-Rao bound
  - phase change material
  - negative results
  - measurement uncertainty
  - GUM
